ntdll: an image that has moved must say so in its own header.

Two paths relocate a PE image and they do not do the same amount of work.
`map_image_view`'s "relocate to dynamic base" block (unix/virtual.c:3486) writes
`OptionalHeader.ImageBase` and then applies the base relocations.
`virtual_relocate_module` (unix/virtual.c:4199), which `load_ntdll` and
`load_ntdll_wow64` call when a builtin cannot have the address it was mapped at,
applies the relocations and stops.

So ntdll can end up at a new address while its own header still names the old
one. `ImageBase` is the documented way to find a loaded module's base from its
headers, which makes reading it an ordinary thing for a program to do -- and on
Windows it is always right, because the loader keeps it right.

ROOT CAUSE OF A CRASH THAT LOOKED LIKE FOUR DIFFERENT BUGS. Resident Evil
Requiem died at its title screen for days. In the session that named it:

    virtual:virtual_relocate_module 0x6fffe30000 -> 0x7fffe30000
    ...
    virtual:virtual_handle_fault unrepaired fault at 0x6fffe3003c, no view covers it
    seh:dispatch_exception code=c0000005 info[1]=0000006FFFE3003C  rbx=0000006fffe3003c

0x6fffe30000 + 0x3c is `e_lfanew` in the DOS header of a module that had moved to
0x7fffe30000. The game's protection walked ntdll's headers, believed
`ImageBase`, and read from where ntdll used to be.

What followed is why this was hard, and it is worth writing down because none of
it points at the cause. The faulting code is protection-generated: no module, no
unwind data. An x86-64 frame with no function entry is unwound as a leaf -- pop a
qword off the stack, call it a return address -- so the handler search cannot
fail, it can only wander. It walked roughly 180 frames of stack *data* (visibly
IEEE-754 doubles), never once reporting "exception data not found", and ended at

    seh:call_seh_handlers invalid frame 54005e (0000000000022000-0000000000420000)
    seh:NtRaiseException Exception frame is not in stack limits => unable to dispatch

0x54005e fails `is_valid_arm64ec_frame` on its first test, alignment. Nothing was
ever offered the exception, so the game drew an unhandled-exception dialog whose
stack said nothing about a bad pointer. Every earlier theory came from reading
that stack.

WHY THIS PLATFORM. Windows randomises ntdll's base once per boot and maps it at
that address in every process, so a cached base is always valid there.
patches/wine/0002 forces PE images into anonymous memory on Android, because
SELinux refuses execmod on a dirtied file mapping, and that patch's own notes
record ntdll being relocated as the normal case here. This is the common path,
not a corner.

The header page is protected separately: SizeOfHeaders covers the DOS stub, the
NT headers and the section table, none of which is inside a section, so the
loop that unprotects sections does not cover it.

A THEORY THIS REPLACES, disproved by measurement rather than by argument: that
ARM64X `VALUE` fixups leave stale absolute addresses behind after a rebase. The
shipped ntdll's dynamic relocation table holds 11 VALUE fixups, none of them
8 bytes and none writing an address inside the image, while all 886 .reloc
entries are DIR64. Nothing stale can come from there.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -4245,6 +4245,60 @@
     while (rel && rel < end - 1 && rel->SizeOfBlock && rel->VirtualAddress < total_size)
         rel = process_relocation_block( (char *)module + rel->VirtualAddress, rel, delta );
 
+    /* Vessel: an image that has moved must say so in its own header.
+     *
+     * The map-time relocation above in map_image_view does exactly two things
+     * when it moves an image -- it writes OptionalHeader.ImageBase and then
+     * applies the base relocations. This path did only the second, so a module
+     * relocated here kept a header claiming the address it no longer occupies,
+     * and every reader of that field got a pointer into nothing.
+     *
+     * Windows has no such state: the in-memory ImageBase always equals the load
+     * address, which is what makes reading it a normal thing for a program to
+     * do. It is also *the* documented way to find a module's base from its
+     * headers, so the field being wrong is not a detail -- it is a lie told to
+     * anything that walks a loaded image.
+     *
+     * MEASURED, and it is what killed Resident Evil Requiem. ntdll is placed at
+     * 0x6ffe30000 in most processes; in the game's process that address was
+     * taken, so it came through here and moved to 0x7fffe30000 while its header
+     * still read 0x6fffe30000. The game's protection walks ntdll's headers, took
+     * that base, and read e_lfanew from it:
+     *
+     *   unrepaired fault at 0x6fffe3003c, no view covers it
+     *
+     * -- 0x6fffe30000 + 0x3c, the DOS header of a module that is not there. What
+     * followed looked like a different bug entirely and cost days: the faulting
+     * code is protection-generated and has no unwind data, so the handler search
+     * treated it as a leaf, walked stack *data* for ~180 frames, produced a
+     * misaligned establisher frame, and gave up --
+     *
+     *   invalid frame 54005e (0000000000022000-0000000000420000)
+     *   Exception frame is not in stack limits => unable to dispatch exception
+     *
+     * -- an unhandled exception dialog whose stack said nothing about the cause.
+     *
+     * The header page is protected separately because it is not one of the
+     * sections the loop above unprotects: SizeOfHeaders covers the DOS stub, the
+     * NT headers and the section table, and none of it is inside a section.
+     */
+    {
+        void *addr = module;
+        SIZE_T size = nt->OptionalHeader.SizeOfHeaders;
+        ULONG old_prot;
+
+        if (!NtProtectVirtualMemory( NtCurrentProcess(), &addr, &size, PAGE_READWRITE, &old_prot ))
+        {
+            if (nt->OptionalHeader.Magic == IMAGE_NT_OPTIONAL_HDR64_MAGIC)
+                ((IMAGE_NT_HEADERS64 *)nt)->OptionalHeader.ImageBase = (ULONG_PTR)module;
+            else
+                ((IMAGE_NT_HEADERS32 *)nt)->OptionalHeader.ImageBase = (ULONG_PTR)module;
+            NtProtectVirtualMemory( NtCurrentProcess(), &addr, &size, old_prot, &old_prot );
+        }
+        else ERR( "could not make the headers of %p writable; its ImageBase still reads %p\n",
+                  module, (void *)image_base );
+    }
+
     for (i = 0; i < nt->FileHeader.NumberOfSections; i++)
     {
         void *addr = (char *)module + sec[i].VirtualAddress;
